WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.016 --> 00:00:05.400
Four-year-old Alexandre Dumas carried his father’s gun up the stairs to the attic.

2
00:00:05.401 --> 00:00:13.279
He had been told that God had taken his father to heaven, so the boy was determined to kill God in revenge for this injustice.

3
00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:21.725
The youngster’s boldness signaled the man he would become—a man of action, who went to war and fought duels to defend his honor.

4
00:00:21.726 --> 00:00:26.392
He was also a man who loved life and was always surrounded by friends.

5
00:00:26.393 --> 00:00:34.777
He was a master storyteller—able to weave adventure into stories that still delight audiences and readers around the world.

6
00:00:34.778 --> 00:00:43.494
Alexandre Dumas was born on July 24, 1802, in the village of Villers-Cotterêts, near Paris, France. His

7
00:00:43.495 --> 00:00:49.587
father, the son of a marquis and of a black slave, was a general in Napoleon’s army. When

8
00:00:49.588 --> 00:00:57.699
Napoleon refused to pay the general’s pension because Alexandre’s father had criticized him, the family became very poor. At

9
00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:04.190
the general’s death, the poverty worsened, and Alexandre’s mother could not afford schooling for her son. But

10
00:01:04.191 --> 00:01:11.246
Alexandre experienced a youth rich in adventure—playing, hunting, and fishing with other young scamps. When

11
00:01:11.247 --> 00:01:17.294
he finally went to school at age ten, he quickly earned the respect of his peers by fighting a bully. He

12
00:01:17.295 --> 00:01:20.906
learned little but developed beautiful penmanship. As

13
00:01:20.907 --> 00:01:34.286
Alexandre grew up, he always tried to live up to the stories of his father, a great warrior who single-handedly defended a bridge against Austrian troops and a man who had the nerve to stand up to Napoleon. In

14
00:01:34.287 --> 00:01:41.007
1823, Dumas moved to Paris, the center of literature and culture in France. He

15
00:01:41.008 --> 00:01:51.097
used his excellent penmanship to obtain a job copying documents for the Duke of Orléans, and in his spare time, he wrote plays and read voraciously. Confident

16
00:01:51.098 --> 00:01:59.808
of his talent as a writer, he said, "I will live by my penmanship now, but the time will come when I shall live by my pen." He wrote

17
00:01:59.809 --> 00:02:09.468
several plays with a friend—two comedies were presented in Paris theaters—but the direction of his writing changed when he saw Shakespeare’s Hamlet . He

18
00:02:09.469 --> 00:02:20.180
liked the English playwright’s use of history and said he felt like "someone blind from birth whose sight is suddenly restored and who discovers a whole new world." The

19
00:02:20.181 --> 00:02:35.226
inspiration for his next play, Henry III and His Court , came from a real event: a nobleman, finding his wife unfaithful to him, gave her the choice to either kill herself or arrange a meeting with her lover so he can be punished.

20
00:02:35.227 --> 00:02:45.118
Dumas developed this incident into a play about the politics of France in the 1500s—full of snappy dialogue, daring heroes, and hateful villains.

21
00:02:45.119 --> 00:02:49.614
Produced in 1829, the show was a huge success.

22
00:02:49.615 --> 00:02:59.260
Dumas became known for his ability to make historical events and characters more exciting and dramatic using his own experiences and vivid imagination.

23
00:02:59.261 --> 00:03:08.715
During the 1830s, newspapers in France printed stories by chapters in successive issues—known as serial novels—to attract readers.

24
00:03:08.716 --> 00:03:17.760
Between 1844 and 1846, Dumas wrote The Three Musketeers and The Count of Monte Cristo in serial form. Packed

25
00:03:17.761 --> 00:03:24.800
with the adventures and rapid-fire dialogue of his plays, these two novels are his most famous works. The

26
00:03:24.801 --> 00:03:35.295
Three Musketeers gave the world the heroes D’Artagnan, Athos, Porthos, and Aramis, as well as the quote "One for all and all for one." Pitting

27
00:03:35.296 --> 00:03:44.170
Dumas’ heroes against the evil Cardinal Richelieu in defense of the queen of France, the book brought seventeenth-century France to life. In

28
00:03:44.171 --> 00:03:54.211
The Count of Monte Cristo , the hero, Edmond Dantès, is falsely accused by a man who wants to marry Dantès’ fiancée and is sent to prison.

29
00:03:54.212 --> 00:03:58.610
There, he learns of a treasure on the island of Monte Cristo.

30
00:03:58.611 --> 00:04:06.874
After a daring escape, he uses the treasure to reward his benefactors and punish those responsible for his misfortunes.

31
00:04:06.875 --> 00:04:18.432
The theme in both books—righting wrongs and obtaining revenge against abuses by the powerful—echoes the writer’s feelings toward the abuses his father suffered at the hands of Napoleon.

32
00:04:18.433 --> 00:04:27.370
Dumas was a prolific writer. During the 1830s, at least one of his plays was produced each year; in some years, four or five.

33
00:04:27.372 --> 00:04:33.077
In one year, 1844, he completed four novels and began a fifth.

34
00:04:33.078 --> 00:04:38.854
Over his lifetime, he wrote about three hundred literary works, including a cookbook. He

35
00:04:38.855 --> 00:04:48.788
worked ten to fourteen hours a day, but was criticized for using other writers to conduct research, develop outlines, and sometimes create first drafts. This

36
00:04:48.789 --> 00:04:55.495
infuriated Dumas. He even dueled with a collaborator who accused him of stealing his work. Dumas’

37
00:04:55.496 --> 00:05:05.950
supporters point out that he was able to write a novel or a play in days without help and that none of his collaborators ever wrote a successful work alone. It

38
00:05:05.951 --> 00:05:14.825
took Dumas’ genius—his special ability to combine action, dialogue, and suspense—to turn history into exciting novels. Dumas

39
00:05:14.826 --> 00:05:22.475
had to work hard because he needed money for his frequent trips abroad, involvement in politics, and rich social life. Dumas

40
00:05:22.476 --> 00:05:32.978
lived outside France for many years; his journeys became the source for popular travel books, in which he wove anecdotes with facts about the various regions he visited. Throughout

41
00:05:32.979 --> 00:05:41.289
his life, he participated in political activities to help the cause of democracy and to try to live up to his father’s memory. For

42
00:05:41.290 --> 00:05:51.347
instance, in 1830, he joined the fight against France’s king, Charles X, and seized more than a ton of explosives for the uprising. In

43
00:05:51.348 --> 00:06:01.340
1860, with his schooner, the Emma , he transported guns for the Italian fight for independence and sailed from port to port, inciting revolt. But

44
00:06:01.341 --> 00:06:05.107
above all, he lived for those he loved. He

45
00:06:05.108 --> 00:06:10.496
had dozens of mistresses and acknowledged three children, each by a different mother. His

46
00:06:10.497 --> 00:06:15.330
son, also named Alexandre Dumas, became a famous playwright. (To

47
00:06:15.331 --> 00:06:25.098
avoid confusion, the elder Alexandre is referred to as Dumas pere , meaning "father," and the younger, as Dumas fils , meaning

48
00:06:25.099 --> 00:06:32.097
"son.") He supported his mistresses and children and always kept a champagne glass full of money ready to aid friends in

49
00:06:32.098 --> 00:06:40.864
need. And he had many friends; he invited six hundred people to celebrate the completion of his extravagant home, the Château of Monte

50
00:06:40.865 --> 00:06:51.255
Cristo! Unfortunately, even Dumas’ substantial income could not keep up with his lavish lifestyle, and he had to sell Monte Cristo at a fraction of its

51
00:06:51.256 --> 00:06:57.406
cost. As the end of his life approached, he was unable to write enough to pay his bills and became

52
00:06:57.407 --> 00:07:05.782
destitute. Dumas’ adventures ended on December 5, 1870, when he died of a stroke at his son’s home in Puys,

53
00:07:05.783 --> 00:07:15.073
France. Alexandre Dumas pere experienced love, war, and intrigue through his travels and friendships with both commoners and royalty.

54
00:07:15.074 --> 00:07:21.492
He made many fortunes and lost them all in pursuit of a meaningful, adventurous life.

55
00:07:21.493 --> 00:07:28.316
He achieved his goal of becoming worthy of his father’s memory, but he wondered whether he would be remembered as a writer.

56
00:07:28.317 --> 00:07:40.979
The answer is clear: over 150 years after his death, his work lives on not only in books but also in films, cartoons, plays, and television programs.

57
00:07:40.980 --> 00:07:50.452
His genius continues to inform audiences and make them laugh and cry, just as his life inspires people to live their own lives to the fullest.

